 ## Balanced automatic playback, preview 146
 
-Mira on iOS now defaults to Balanced automatic source selection using a local, profile-scoped choice. It does not overwrite the cloud playback mode used by other Nuvio clients. The existing manual, first-source and regex choices remain in Playback settings. Phone viewing targets 1080p; iPad viewing may prefer a modest 2160p copy. This is a device-class heuristic, not hardware codec detection or a network speed test.
+Mira on iOS defaults to Balanced automatic source selection using a local, profile-scoped choice. It does not overwrite the cloud playback mode used by other Nuvio clients. The existing manual, first-source and regex choices remain in Playback settings. Phone viewing targets 1080p; iPad viewing may prefer a modest 2160p copy. Hardware codec detection was added in the October 13 preview described below; this is not a network speed test.
 
 Balanced ranks playable candidates by resolution, direct/cached readiness, file size and codec, with penalties for huge files, remuxes, AV1 and obvious sample/camera labels. Structured size metadata takes priority over text. Duplicate URLs are removed from the candidate queue while different required headers remain distinct. Enabled-source filters are preserved. Selection compares responses for at least three seconds while providers are loading. A ready device-sized copy can then launch; oversized or lower-resolution candidates wait for the remaining providers, up to a 15-second overall selection fallback to the manual picker if no ready source is available. That bound covers selection, not later resolver/player startup or buffering. New source responses cannot restart automatic selection after Choose source is pressed. Existing resolver preparation failures can advance through the remaining ranked queue; player errors do not yet automatically cycle every remaining source.
 
